t(6, 2450, """Chickens practice the behavior of eating their own droppings, a matter well known to breeders. You may notice a chicken pecking at its own droppings or the droppings of other animals, and there are several reasons that drive it to do so:
Searching for insects: Often the chicken searches the feces for larvae or insects rich in protein and fats that live inside it, in order to eat them.
Nutritional deficiency: A chicken may eat its feces if it suffers from a deficiency in some mineral salts, vitamins, or""")

t(7, 160, """protein in its feed, so it tries to make up for this deficiency.
Hunger or boredom: In cases of severe overcrowding or when the amount of feed is short, the chicken may resort to this behavior to satisfy its hunger or merely for "amusement" and to get rid of boredom in cramped environments.
Undigested food remains: Chickens sometimes prefer the feces of herbivorous animals (such as cows) because it contains partly digested plant parts and seeds that are easy for them to eat.

Is this harmful?
Yes, this behavior increases the risk of diseases spreading among chickens, such as coccidiosis, salmonella or intestinal worms, because parasite eggs and bacteria spread through droppings. And flesh that grew from droppings? Your own understanding is enough!!""")

